      "params": {
        "item": {
          "clientId": null,
          "content": [
            {
              "text": "{\"problem\": {\"theorem\": \"Define $f_{n}:[0,1] \\\\rightarrow \\\\mathbb{R}$ by the equation $f_{n}(x)=x^{n}$. Show that the sequence $\\\\left(f_{n}\\\\right)$ does not converge uniformly.\", \"assumptions\": [], \"domain\": \"topology\", \"explicit_subgoals\": []}, \"candidate_text\": \"For each x∈[0,1), we have x^n→0, while f_n(1)=1 for every n. Thus the pointwise limit is\\n\\nf(x)={0 if 0≤x<1, 1 if x=1}.\\n\\nAssume that (f_n) converges uniformly. Since uniform convergence implies pointwise convergence, its uniform limit must be f. Fix ε=1/4. For every N∈Z_+, take n=N and x=2^{-1/n}∈[0,1). Then f(x)=0, but\\n\\n|f_n(x)−f(x)|=x^n=1/2>ε.\\n\\nHence no N can satisfy the uniform-convergence condition. Therefore (f_n) does not converge uniformly on [0,1].\"}",
              "text_elements": [],
              "type": "text"
            }
          ],
          "id": "01a094c5-1aef-7493-94e9-3ca9b852b8c0",
          "type": "userMessage"
        },
        "startedAtMs": 1789202340591,
        "threadId": "01a094c5-1207-78d2-8882-cbb3a3bd58c0",
        "turnId": "01a094c5-1222-74b1-9771-59cde22cc24b"
      }
    },
    {
